MoCoLinux design note: defect 3 -- asynchronous cooperative block I/O
=====================================================================

Status: design, ready to implement. No code has been written into the tree.
Author's basis: read of the real sources listed under "Sources" at the end.
All line numbers are as of the read on 2026-08-03.


0. TL;DR and the two corrections to the target design
-----------------------------------------------------

The defect is real and the target shape (submit now, complete later, off the
guest CPU) is correct. Two things in the proposed mechanism are wrong for THIS
codebase and are corrected here; a third is simplified.

  (1) Workers cannot be `co_os_thread_start`. That helper
      (src/colinux/os/winnt/user/misc.c:240) is a user-mode `CreateThread` in
      the DAEMON process. The block transfer is done in the KERNEL DRIVER:
      co_cobd_request (src/colinux/kernel/cobd.c:212) -> co_os_bdev_rw
      (src/colinux/os/winnt/kernel/lowlevel/blockio.c:136) -> ZwReadFile/
      ZwWriteFile against a kernel handle, at PASSIVE_LEVEL, inside the KBOOT
      ioctl thread. The workers MUST be kernel system threads
      (PsCreateSystemThread), which do not exist yet and must be added as a
      small os/winnt/kernel helper. This is the single most important finding.

  (2) Completion delivery: choose option B (drain on the existing cooperative
      tick / idle / exit-to-user hooks), NOT a new injected IRQ vector
      (option A). Reasoning in section 5. Short version: the COPIC injector
      (co_arch_inject_tick, switch.c:3161) can only deliver over a ring-3
      frame and only after first idle -- a completion IRQ would inherit exactly
      those constraints, so it would NOT be able to complete into a guest that
      is busy in the kernel, which is the only case where A could theoretically
      beat B. B already achieves <=1 ms latency with essentially zero new host
      assembly and no new guest entry path.

  (3) Simplification: do NOT put a submission ring in guest .bss. Submission is
      already an atomic, cheap, correct handoff through the passage-page params
      under preempt_disable (the guest does this today, cobd.c in the patch,
      cobd_io_sg). Reuse it: the guest writes the same params plus a tag and
      crosses; the host COPIES the request into a driver-side work queue and
      returns immediately (no inline I/O). Only the COMPLETION path needs a ring
      in guest memory, and it mirrors the net RX ring. This removes a whole
      guest-side ring, its full/empty arithmetic, and a second new guest symbol,
      for no measurable cost -- the crossing was never the freeze; the inline
      ZwReadFile was.

Net effect: submit = one cheap (~3.6 us) crossing that returns immediately;
transfer happens on kernel worker threads; completions land in a guest .bss
ring and are reaped on the tick the guest already takes. The guest scheduler,
cotimer, console kthread and systemd watchdogs keep running throughout.


1. The defect, precisely
------------------------

Guest side (patch/7.1.5/current-tree-snapshot.diff, drivers/block/cobd.c):
  - cobd_queue_rq builds a scatter list into the request's PDU
    (blk_mq_rq_to_pdu), then calls cobd_io_sg, then unconditionally
    blk_mq_end_request(req, status) and returns BLK_STS_OK.
  - cobd_io_sg does preempt_disable(); writes params[51..56]; cobd_switch(
    CO_OPERATION_BLOCK_IO); reads params[56] as the result; preempt_enable().
    The switch does not return until the host has finished the whole transfer.

Host side (src/colinux/arch/x86_64/switch.c, monitor loop):
  - At switch.c:4046, on op == CO_OPERATION_BLOCK_IO, the loop calls
    co_cobd_request_sg (cobd.c:100) INLINE, which walks every descriptor and
    every page and performs the full ZwReadFile/ZwWriteFile loop
    (co_cobd_request, cobd.c:212 -> co_os_bdev_rw), then sets params[56] and
    `continue`s -- i.e. re-enters the guest -- only after all I/O is done.

Because the guest is single-CPU and the monitor thread is CPU-pinned
(co_os_pin_cpu at manager.c:951), the guest makes NO progress of any kind for
the entire duration of host disk I/O. Measured: 8.7 s of guest CPU over 5 m 14 s
wall during mke2fs+pacman (~97% blocked), tripping systemd's 3-minute service
watchdogs (journald, userdbd SIGABRT'd).

Note this is a DIFFERENT and unrelated thing from
co_os_file_block_async_read_write in src/colinux/os/winnt/kernel/block.c:136 --
that is legacy coLinux message-based block I/O (co_monitor_file_block_*
operations, CO_DEVICE_BLOCK messages) and is NOT on the cobd path. Do not
confuse the two; the cobd path is cobd.c + blockio.c only.


2. Facts that constrain the design (read, not assumed)
------------------------------------------------------

2a. The passage page and its params array.
    Definition: patch/x86_64-state-struct.diff, `typedef struct
    co_arch_passage_page`. first_page is 0x1000 bytes; within it:
      host_state    @ 0x740  (co_arch_state_stack_t, 0x140)
      linuxvm_state @ 0x880
      operation     @ 0x9c0
      params[0]     @ 0x9c8   -> params[n] at 0x9c8 + 8*n
    So params runs to index (0x1000-0x9c8)/8 - 1 = 198. Current occupancy
    (switch.c comments + code, and cobd.c in the patch):
      [0..7] fault/counter/regsum; [8..15] guest GDT scratch (also aliased at
      [64..73] for the real guest GDT, see switch.c:1679-1685);
      [16..18] vector/errcode/cr2; [19] switch entry; [20..27] kcall;
      [28] guest frame; [29] step; [30] batch; [31..46] trace; [47] trace idx;
      [48] slept-ticks bank; [49] host monotonic 100ns; [50] host wallclock;
      [51..56] block I/O (unit, offset, count, sg_pa, write, result).
    FREE and usable immediately: [57..63] and [74..198]. We use exactly one new
    slot, [57] = tag. No struct field is added, so NO new offset assertion is
    strictly required (section 10 explains what to add anyway, and why).

2b. How guest kernel symbols reach the host. The daemon resolves them from the
    vmlinux ELF by name (src/colinux/user/elf_load.c, co_get_symbol_by_name)
    and passes their VAs in the KBOOT ioctl params. See elf_load.c:2202
    (co_colinux_passage_page), :2220-2234 (co_colinux_console_io,
    co_colinux_net_io). manager.c:919-938 copies params into co_arch_boot_t and
    calls co_console_set_address / co_net_set_address. This is the exact channel
    the completion ring's address will travel through.

2c. How the host reaches guest memory. co_kload_read / co_kload_write
    (kload.h:118-121) walk the guest tables; co_kload_frame_va(pfn)
    (kload.h:84) maps a guest physical frame to a host VA. co_cobd_request
    already uses co_kload_frame_va per page. These are callable from any
    PASSIVE_LEVEL thread, and the net pump ALREADY calls co_kload_write
    concurrently with the monitor loop from a different process (net.c). Our
    workers are threads in the SAME process (the driver), which is strictly
    simpler.

2d. The net rings are the pattern to mirror (guest:
    drivers/net/conet_colinux.c struct co_net_io; host: src/colinux/kernel/
    net.c). Key properties we copy for the completion ring:
      - a plain EXPORT_SYMBOL'd .bss object, free-running masked indices,
        full == head-tail == size;
      - producer writes payload, then a store to the head index; on x86 store
        order is the barrier (net.c:153-155, co_net_put); consumer reads head,
        smp_rmb(), reads payload, then stores tail (conet co_net_rx_drain);
      - the address is guarded by a mutex (net_lock, net.c:44) ONLY to protect
        the address pointer against teardown, not the ring itself.

2e. Injection reality (why option A buys nothing). co_arch_inject_tick
    (switch.c:3161) refuses unless: addresses present; out->idle_yields>0
    (guest has idled at least once, :3197); guest virtual IF set (:3217);
    saved frame RFLAGS.IF set (:3241); AND the interrupted frame is ring 3
    (:3282, the swapgs/SYSCALL hazard documented at :3244-3283). So the host
    can only vector a synthetic interrupt into USERSPACE frames. A dedicated
    completion vector would have the identical restriction and therefore could
    not deliver into a kernel-mode-busy guest either -- the one scenario an IRQ
    could beat polling. Meanwhile the guest already drains time on the tick
    (HZ=1000), at idle (co_cpu_idle), and on EVERY return to userspace
    (__exit_to_user_mode_prepare, patch line ~2835). Those are our drain points.

2f. Kernel threading/sync already available in the driver:
      - co_os_mutex (src/colinux/os/kernel/mutex.h, impl winnt/kernel/lowlevel/
        mutex.c) -- PASSIVE_LEVEL mutex, used by net.
      - co_os_wait (src/colinux/os/kernel/wait.h, impl winnt/kernel/lowlevel/
        wait.c) -- a KEVENT SynchronizationEvent with sleep/wakeup.
    MISSING: a kernel system-thread create/join. Must be added (section 9).

2g. Teardown discipline. co_arch_boot_loaded runs the whole monitor loop and
    then frees the stack and passage page (switch.c:4729-4736); guest RAM is
    freed later at KLOAD_END / unload via co_kload_free, serialized by the
    teardown lock (kload.h:127-133). Driver unload calls co_arch_boot_abort
    (switch.c:3396) and waits on co_arch_boot_running (:3401). Everything the
    workers touch (guest RAM, the passage page's guest CR3) is alive for the
    duration of co_arch_boot_loaded and only freed after it returns. Therefore
    joining workers INSIDE co_arch_boot_loaded, before it returns, closes the
    race completely -- stronger than the net pump's cross-process guarantee.


3. Mechanism
------------

3.1 Submission (guest -> host), reusing the params handoff
----------------------------------------------------------

Guest cobd_queue_rq, async branch (see 3.6 for the mode gate):
  - blk_mq_start_request(req) as today.
  - Build the SG list into cmd->sg[] (blk_mq_rq_to_pdu) exactly as today. The
    PDU stays allocated and stable until we complete the request, because the
    request stays outstanding -- this is what lets the host read the SG list
    later from a worker.
  - preempt_disable(); write the SAME params the sync path writes, PLUS the tag:
        params[51] = unit
        params[52] = offset (byte)
        params[53] = count (num sg descriptors)
        params[54] = sg_pa  (virt_to_phys(cmd->sg))
        params[55] = write?1:0
        params[57] = req->tag           (NEW: the blk-mq tag, 0..15)
        params[56] = ~0ULL              (host overwrites: 0=accepted, 1=busy)
    cobd_switch(CO_OPERATION_BLOCK_IO);
    accepted = (params[56] == 0);
    preempt_enable();
  - if (!accepted)  ->  the driver-side host queue was full: return
    BLK_STS_DEV_RESOURCE so blk-mq requeues and retries (backpressure, 3.7).
    Do NOT end the request.
  - if accepted     ->  return BLK_STS_OK WITHOUT blk_mq_end_request. The
    request is now outstanding; it will be completed by the completion drain
    (3.4) when its tag comes back.

Host monitor loop, at switch.c:4046 (op == CO_OPERATION_BLOCK_IO), async mode:
  - Read the six/seven words from params.
  - Call co_cobd_async_submit(manager, unit, offset, sg_pa, count, write, tag).
    This copies them into a driver-side work node, appends it to the submission
    queue under a mutex, signals the worker wait channel, and returns a bool.
  - params[56] = accepted ? 0 : 1;  out->block_requests++ (keep the heartbeat).
  - `continue;` -- return to the guest immediately. No inline I/O.

Why params handoff and not a guest submission ring: the handoff is already
atomic and correct (preempt_disable makes the param-write + crossing
uninterruptible on the single guest CPU; the host copies before returning, so
param reuse by the next request is safe). It needs no ring memory, no
full/empty arithmetic in the guest, and no second guest symbol. The crossing
cost (~3.6 us) is three orders of magnitude below the I/O it replaces inline.

3.2 The driver-side submission queue and worker pool (host, in-kernel)
----------------------------------------------------------------------

New host module, portable part: src/colinux/kernel/cobd_async.c (+ .h). It owns:
  - a fixed array of work nodes (a slot pool), each:
        struct co_cobd_work { int unit; unsigned long long offset;
                              unsigned long long sg_pa; unsigned int count;
                              bool_t write; unsigned int tag;
                              volatile int in_use; };
    Size the pool >= total possible outstanding = COBD_MAX_UNITS(4) *
    queue_depth(16) = 64. Use 64 (or round to 128 for headroom). A full pool is
    the backpressure signal (3.7).
  - a submission FIFO (indices into the pool, or an intrusive singly-linked
    list of nodes) protected by co_os_mutex `sub_lock`.
  - a co_os_wait `sub_wait` the workers sleep on when the FIFO is empty.
  - a volatile `stopping` flag.
  - the completion-ring address + `comp_lock` (co_os_mutex), set/cleared like
    net_io_va (3.3).
  - N worker kernel threads (section 9 helper). Recommended N: one per attached
    unit, created lazily, or simply a small fixed pool. See 3.8 for the count
    rationale; ONE worker is correct and sufficient for the fix -- start there.

Worker loop (each thread, PASSIVE_LEVEL):
    for (;;) {
        node = dequeue(sub_lock);            // NULL if empty
        if (!node) {
            if (stopping) return;            // exit -> thread terminates
            co_os_wait_sleep(sub_wait);      // woken by submit or by stop
            continue;
        }
        rc = co_cobd_request_sg(manager, node->unit, node->offset,
                                node->sg_pa, node->count, node->write);
        result = CO_OK(rc) ? 0 : 1;
        co_cobd_complete(node->unit, node->tag, result);   // 3.3
        free_node(node);
    }

co_cobd_request_sg / co_cobd_request are reused UNCHANGED -- they already walk
guest memory by co_kload_frame_va and validate every descriptor and bound
(cobd.c:100-291). They are pure functions of (manager, params) with no global
state except cobd_unit[], which is read-only during a run. Running them on a
worker thread instead of the monitor thread is safe: co_kload_frame_va is a
pure arithmetic map over stable allocations, and distinct workers touch
distinct guest pages (different requests) and distinct file offsets.

3.3 The completion ring (host -> guest), a new guest .bss symbol
----------------------------------------------------------------

New guest object, drivers/block/cobd.c, EXPORT_SYMBOL'd, mirroring co_net_io:

    #define CO_COBD_COMP_SLOTS   128        /* power of two, >= 64 */
    struct co_cobd_comp {
        __u32 unit;
        __u32 tag;
        __u32 result;                       /* 0 = OK, 1 = I/O error */
        __u32 pad;
    };                                       /* 16 bytes, fixed size */

    struct co_cobd_io {
        volatile __u32 enabled;             /* host sets 1 at boot if async on */
        volatile __u32 comp_head;           /* host writes (producer) */
        volatile __u32 comp_tail;           /* guest writes (consumer) */
        volatile __u32 pad;
        struct co_cobd_comp comp[CO_COBD_COMP_SLOTS];
    };
    struct co_cobd_io co_colinux_cobd_io;
    EXPORT_SYMBOL(co_colinux_cobd_io);

This is a SLOT ring (fixed-size records), not a byte ring -- simpler than net
because completion records are fixed size. Indices free-run and are masked by
(CO_COBD_COMP_SLOTS-1); full == head-tail == CO_COBD_COMP_SLOTS. The ring can
never actually fill because outstanding <= 64 < 128 and each outstanding
request yields exactly one completion, but the producer still checks and treats
full as a hard error (it would mean the guest stopped draining, i.e. a bug).

Host ABI offsets into struct co_cobd_io (declared in cobd_async.c, mirroring
net.c's CO_NIO_* block, must match the guest struct which is declared ABI):
    ENABLED   0x00
    COMP_HEAD 0x04
    COMP_TAIL 0x08
    COMP      0x10, stride 0x10, CO_COBD_COMP_SLOTS entries

Host producer, co_cobd_complete(unit, tag, result):
    co_os_mutex_acquire(comp_lock);
    if (!cobd_io_va) { release; return; }           // torn down
    read comp_head, comp_tail via co_kload_read;
    if (comp_head - comp_tail >= CO_COBD_COMP_SLOTS) { // "cannot happen"
        co_debug_error("cobd: completion ring full"); release; return; }
    off = comp_head & (SLOTS-1);
    write {unit,tag,result,0} to cobd_io_va + COMP + off*0x10 via co_kload_write;
    // payload is fully written before the index that announces it; x86 store
    // order is the barrier, exactly as co_net_put relies on (net.c:153-155).
    write (comp_head + 1) to cobd_io_va + COMP_HEAD via co_kload_write;
    co_os_mutex_release(comp_lock);

The mutex serializes MULTIPLE WORKERS producing into one ring (3.8). One guest
consumer, no lock needed on the guest side.

3.4 Guest completion drain (consumer)
-------------------------------------

New guest function in drivers/block/cobd.c:

    void co_cobd_drain_completions(void)
    {
        struct co_cobd_io *io = &co_colinux_cobd_io;
        u32 head = io->comp_head;
        u32 tail = io->comp_tail;

        while (tail != head) {
            struct co_cobd_comp *c;
            struct cobd_dev *dev;
            struct request *rq;

            smp_rmb();                       // pairs with host store order
            c = &io->comp[tail & (CO_COBD_COMP_SLOTS - 1)];
            if (c->unit < COBD_MAX_UNITS &&
                (dev = &cobd_devs[c->unit])->disk &&
                c->tag < dev->tag_set.queue_depth) {
                rq = blk_mq_tag_to_rq(dev->tag_set.tags[0], c->tag);
                if (rq && blk_mq_request_started(rq))
                    blk_mq_end_request(rq,
                        c->result ? BLK_STS_IOERR : BLK_STS_OK);
            }
            tail++;
        }
        io->comp_tail = tail;                // x86 store order: after the loads
    }

Notes:
  - Use the numeric blk-mq tag as the cookie and resolve it with
    blk_mq_tag_to_rq, NOT a raw struct request* passed through the host. A
    corrupted completion record then cannot turn into an arbitrary kernel
    pointer; the worst case is a NULL or a request that is not started, both
    handled. nr_hw_queues==1, so tags[0] is the only tag map.
  - blk_mq_end_request is legal from any context including the injected-IRQ
    context of sysvec_co_timer and the irqs-disabled context of drain_time.
    (Alternative, more idiomatic: set a status field in cmd->pdu and call
    blk_mq_complete_request with a registered .complete op; equivalent here,
    and blk_mq_end_request is fewer moving parts. Either is acceptable.)
  - Double completion is prevented by the protocol: the host appends exactly
    one completion per accepted submission, and blk_mq_request_started guards a
    tag that was already ended and recycled. This directly avoids the recurring
    0x50-class double-completion the memory notes warn about, on the guest side.

Hook points (all already exist in the patch):
  - sysvec_co_timer (patch line ~1418): after the take_ticks loop, call
    co_cobd_drain_completions(). Fires up to HZ=1000 times/s over ring-3.
  - co_colinux_drain_time (patch line ~1429): after its take_ticks work. This
    is called from co_cpu_idle (idle boundary) and from
    __exit_to_user_mode_prepare (every syscall/exception return, patch ~2835).
  Practically: add ONE call inside co_colinux_drain_time, and one inside
  sysvec_co_timer. To keep drivers/block/cobd.c decoupled from
  arch/x86/kernel/process.c, expose a registered function pointer set at cobd
  init:  process.c holds `void (*co_cobd_drain)(void)` (default NULL), calls it
  if non-NULL; cobd_init sets it. (Mirrors how other cooperative bits are wired
  by symbol.) A direct extern also works since cobd is obj-y and built-in.

Latency: a task waiting on a read goes to sleep -> co_cpu_idle -> host sleeps
1 ms -> re-enters -> drain_time -> co_cobd_drain_completions -> blk_mq_end ->
waiter woken. Worst case ~1 ms; typically less because exit-to-user drains too.

3.5 Passage-page params used
----------------------------
No new struct field. Uses existing free slot params[57] for the tag, and the
existing params[51..56] with params[56] now meaning "accepted(0)/busy(1)"
instead of "io result(0/1)". Guest and host both change meaning together.

3.6 Mode negotiation (so the sync path survives behind a flag)
--------------------------------------------------------------
A single host->guest enable byte, co_colinux_cobd_io.enabled:
  - Daemon gets a new flag, --async-cobd (default OFF during bring-up, ON once
    proven). It sets a new KBOOT param `async_cobd` (0/1) and resolves
    co_colinux_cobd_io -> cobd_io_va, both handed to the driver.
  - Host: if async_cobd && cobd_io_va, before entering the guest it writes
    enabled=1 into the ring (co_kload_write) and starts the worker pool; else
    it leaves enabled=0 and never starts workers.
  - Guest cobd_queue_rq reads co_colinux_cobd_io.enabled ONCE (cache in a
    static bool at first call, or read per call -- it is one word):
      enabled  -> async path (3.1): submit, return outstanding.
      !enabled -> the EXISTING sync path unchanged: cobd_io_sg reads params[56]
                  as the result and blk_mq_end_request inline.
Both host and guest branch on the same fact, so they can never disagree. With
the flag off the behaviour is byte-for-byte today's, which is the tested
fallback and the A/B baseline.

3.7 Backpressure
----------------
The only place submission can fail for flow-control reasons is the driver-side
work-node pool being full. Because outstanding <= 64 and the pool is >= 64,
this should never happen; if it does, the host returns "busy" (params[56]=1)
and the guest returns BLK_STS_DEV_RESOURCE, which tells blk-mq to requeue and
retry after a short delay -- the correct, lossless response. Never drop or
error a request for lack of a slot. (Contrast the net TX ring, which drops,
because ethernet tolerates loss; block I/O does not.)

3.8 Worker count
----------------
The backing handle is opened FILE_SYNCHRONOUS_IO_NONALERT (blockio.c:112), so
the I/O manager serializes all requests on a single file object regardless of
how many threads call it. Consequences:
  - More than one worker PER UNIT buys nothing: they serialize on the handle.
  - Different units have different handles and CAN proceed in parallel.
Recommendation:
  - Bring-up: ONE worker thread total. It fully fixes the freeze (the point is
    that I/O is off the guest CPU, not that it is parallel) and is the simplest
    thing to get right.
  - Production: a small fixed pool, ideally sized to the number of ATTACHED
    units (co_cobd_present), giving cross-unit parallelism with no wasted
    threads. Do NOT try to parallelize a single unit; that would require
    reopening handles without FILE_SYNCHRONOUS_IO and an event-per-request wait,
    a much larger change that this defect does not need.
A single global submission FIFO feeding the pool is fine; per-unit ordering is
not required by the block layer for correctness, and the completion ring
carries the tag so out-of-order completion is expected and handled.


4. Concurrency analysis
-----------------------

Threads in play during a run:
  T0  the KBOOT/monitor thread (CPU-pinned), running co_arch_boot_loaded.
  T1..Tn  cobd worker kernel threads (PASSIVE_LEVEL, not pinned).
  Tx  possibly another process's thread doing KSTOP/unload -> co_arch_boot_abort.
  (The guest itself is single-CPU and runs only while T0 is inside the switch.)

4a. Submission queue: single producer (T0), multiple consumers (T1..Tn).
    Guard the FIFO and the node pool with `sub_lock` (co_os_mutex). Signal
    `sub_wait` on every enqueue. Standard, no lock ordering issues (sub_lock is
    a leaf; workers never hold it across co_cobd_request_sg or co_kload_*).

4b. Completion ring: multiple producers (T1..Tn), single consumer (the guest).
    Guard the host producer with `comp_lock` so two workers cannot interleave a
    record write with a head advance. The guest consumer needs no lock (one
    consumer) and pairs with the producer via smp_rmb / x86 store order exactly
    as conet does. comp_lock is a leaf held only across the co_kload_write pair.

4c. Memory ordering. Host producer: write the 16-byte record, THEN write
    comp_head. On x86 (both host and guest are x86-64) stores are not reordered
    with other stores, so no explicit host barrier is needed beyond ordinary C
    statement order through co_kload_write -- this is the identical argument
    net.c:153-155 makes and it has been validated on hardware. Guest consumer:
    read comp_head, smp_rmb(), read the record, process, then store comp_tail
    (its store cannot pass the preceding loads on x86, so the host will not
    overwrite a record still being read -- same as conet co_net_rx_drain).
    The `enabled` word is written once by the host before any guest I/O and read
    by the guest; no ordering subtlety (it gates the very first submission).

4d. Teardown race (the important one). Ordering inside co_arch_boot_loaded:
      setup:   if async: co_cobd_async_start(manager, cobd_io_va)  // create
               locks/wait, spawn workers, they begin serving.
      loop:    ... enqueue on BLOCK_IO ...
      end:     (loop breaks on idle cap / TERMINATE / abort / fatal fault)
      STOP:    co_cobd_async_stop():
                 stopping = 1;
                 co_os_wait_wakeup(sub_wait) for each worker (or broadcast);
                 join every worker (KeWaitForSingleObject on the thread object);
                 co_os_mutex_acquire(comp_lock); cobd_io_va = 0;
                   co_os_mutex_release(comp_lock);   // no late producer
                 drain/free the FIFO and pool; destroy sub_lock, comp_lock,
                 sub_wait.
      then:    existing co_os_free_exec_pages / stack free (switch.c:4729).
    Because stop() JOINS before co_arch_boot_loaded returns, and guest RAM /
    guest CR3 are only freed AFTER it returns (KLOAD_END / co_kload_free,
    kload.h:127), no worker can walk freed tables. This is the discipline the
    memory notes call KREAD teardown-gap, satisfied by construction: the readers
    are in-process and joined on the same thread that owns the guest's lifetime.
    A worker blocked in a synchronous ZwReadFile at stop time delays the join by
    at most one request (bounded, low ms); it cannot be cancelled mid-call and
    must not be (that is the "never taskkill / unload the driver first" rule --
    here unload already waits on co_arch_boot_running, and stop() completes the
    join before the loop clears boot_loop_active at switch.c:4693). Move the
    boot_loop_active=0 store to AFTER co_cobd_async_stop() so unload cannot
    proceed until workers are gone.

4e. Abort path. co_arch_boot_abort (Tx) only sets boot_loop_abort; the loop
    observes it and breaks; stop() then runs on T0 as above. Workers are never
    torn down from Tx. No cross-thread thread-kill anywhere.


5. Completion delivery: A vs B, decision
-----------------------------------------

Option A: host injects a new COPIC vector (say CO_COBD_VECTOR) when it appends
completions, guest handles it in a new DEFINE_IDTENTRY_SYSVEC that drains.

Option B (RECOMMENDED): guest drains the completion ring on the hooks it
already runs -- sysvec_co_timer (HZ=1000), co_colinux_drain_time at idle, and
__exit_to_user_mode_prepare on every return to userspace.

Decision: B.

  Latency: B is <=1 ms in the wait-for-I/O case (idle re-entry cadence) and
  effectively immediate whenever the guest is returning to userspace, which
  under any real workload is constantly. A would be "immediate" only in the
  cases where injection is permitted -- and those are a STRICT SUBSET of "guest
  in userspace", which B already covers via exit-to-user. A cannot deliver into
  a kernel-busy guest (2e), so it has no latency win in the only case B is slow.

  Code size / risk: A requires a new guest idtentry, a new host injector
  mirroring co_arch_inject_tick's frame surgery (switch.c:3161-3317) with all
  of its swapgs/SYSCALL-window hazards (the exact class of bug that triple
  faulted under Steam, :3277-3280), a new vector constant, and per-crossing
  injection accounting. B requires two one-line calls into an existing drain and
  a fixed-size ring. B is a fraction of the risk and touches none of the parts
  of the port that have historically triple-faulted.

  When A would ever be worth it: only if a future workload showed real block
  latency dominated by a guest that is CPU-bound in the KERNEL for long
  stretches with no syscalls and no idle. That is pathological and, even then,
  A could not help (injection is userspace-only). So A is not merely unnecessary
  now, it is architecturally unable to beat B under the port's current injection
  constraints. Revisit only if those constraints change (e.g. a kernel-frame
  injection path is ever built for other reasons).


6. Error propagation
---------------------

  - A transfer that fails in co_cobd_request_sg / co_os_bdev_rw returns
    non-OK; the worker sets result=1 and appends {unit, tag, 1} to the
    completion ring. The guest drain calls blk_mq_end_request(rq, BLK_STS_IOERR)
    for that exact tag. This preserves today's semantics (out->block_errors is
    still counted -- increment it in the worker or in co_cobd_async_complete so
    the heartbeat/report is unchanged).
  - A submission the host refuses for flow control (pool full) returns busy ->
    BLK_STS_DEV_RESOURCE -> requeue (not an error).
  - A submission that is structurally invalid (bad unit, count out of range) is
    still caught by co_cobd_request_sg's existing validation on the worker and
    becomes a per-tag BLK_STS_IOERR, same as a media error. The guest-side
    param handoff itself cannot fail once accepted.
  - Ring desync defence: if the guest drain ever reads comp_head-comp_tail >
    CO_COBD_COMP_SLOTS, or a record with unit>=COBD_MAX_UNITS, it logs
    (net_err_ratelimited style) and resets comp_tail=comp_head, matching conet's
    resync stance. This is a bug tripwire, not expected weather.


7. Incremental bring-up plan
----------------------------

Each step builds and is testable on hardware; the box auto power-cycles, so
these are poll-and-observe, not ask.

  Step 0 (no behaviour change): add the guest struct co_cobd_io + EXPORT_SYMBOL
    with enabled defaulted 0; add co_cobd_drain_completions and wire the two
    guest hooks (they no-op while the ring is empty and enabled==0). Add the
    daemon symbol resolution + KBOOT param + host cobd_io_va setter, but leave
    async_cobd default OFF. Result: identical to today; proves the plumbing
    compiles, the symbol resolves, and the drain is harmless.

  Step 1 (host worker infra, still sync): add the kernel-thread helper
    (section 9), cobd_async.c with start/stop/submit/complete and ONE worker,
    and co_cobd_async_start/stop calls in co_arch_boot_loaded -- but keep
    async_cobd OFF so submit is never called and workers idle-sleep and are
    joined cleanly at stop. Proves thread lifecycle and teardown/join under real
    runs and under stop.bat / unload, with zero I/O behaviour change.

  Step 2 (enable async behind the flag): turn on --async-cobd. Host writes
    enabled=1, monitor loop routes BLOCK_IO to co_cobd_async_submit, guest takes
    the async branch. Test matrix: mount, dd read/write (throughput vs the 140
    MB/s sync baseline), mke2fs + pacman install (the workload that tripped the
    watchdogs -- confirm guest CPU% and that journald/userdbd no longer SIGABRT),
    and a forced I/O error (detach/bad offset) to confirm BLK_STS_IOERR reaches
    the right tag. Flip the flag off to A/B against the known-good sync path at
    any time.

  Step 3 (scale workers): if cross-unit throughput matters, grow to one worker
    per attached unit. Pure host-side change, no ABI impact.


