9. The kernel-thread helper (mandatory new primitive)
-----------------------------------------------------

There is no PsCreateSystemThread anywhere in the tree today (grep confirms).
Add, in the winnt KERNEL tree (not user):

    void* co_os_kthread_start(void (*func)(void*), void* arg);
    void  co_os_kthread_join(void* thread);   // waits + drops the ref

Implementation sketch (winnt kernel):
    - trampoline: DWORD/VOID NTAPI that calls func(arg) then
      PsTerminateSystemThread(STATUS_SUCCESS).
    - start: PsCreateSystemThread(&h, THREAD_ALL_ACCESS, NULL, NULL, NULL,
      trampoline, ctx); ObReferenceObjectByHandle(h, ..., *PsThreadType, KernelMode,
      &object, NULL); ZwClose(h); return object.
    - join: KeWaitForSingleObject(object, Executive, KernelMode, FALSE, NULL);
      ObDereferenceObject(object).
Keeping the KTHREAD object reference (not the handle) is what makes the join
race-free against the thread exiting. This mirrors the user helper's contract
(os/user/misc.h:60-72) but with kernel primitives. All calls are PASSIVE_LEVEL,
which the KBOOT ioctl thread and the workers are.


10. Passage-page ABI / build-time offset assertions
----------------------------------------------------

  - NO new passage-page STRUCT FIELD and therefore NO change to the
    co_arch_passage_page layout in patch/x86_64-state-struct.diff. We consume
    the already-existing free slot params[57] (offset 0xb90) and reinterpret
    params[56] (0xb88). The offset-assertion block at switch.c:1412-1429 asserts
    specific indices against CO_PP_*_N literals; none of those indices move, so
    that block is untouched.
  - RECOMMENDED additions in the port's own style (defensive, cheap):
      * In drivers/block/cobd.c add compile-time checks that
        sizeof(struct co_cobd_comp)==16 and offsetof(struct co_cobd_io, comp)==
        0x10 and that CO_COBD_COMP_SLOTS is a power of two -- because the host's
        CO_COBD_* offset literals in cobd_async.c are hand-written against this
        layout, exactly the situation net.c documents as "declared ABI" (net.c:
        21-22). A BUILD_BUG_ON in the guest and a `typedef char [...] ? 1 : -1`
        in the host keep the two definitions from drifting.
      * Add a symmetry note/assert that params[57] and the [51..56] block are
        the SAME indices the guest cobd.c hardcodes via CO_PP_PARAM(n); those
        are literals on both sides (guest: patch cobd.c CO_PP_PARAMS 0x9c8; host:
        params[] base 0x9c8) and there is no shared header, so a comment cross-
        referencing both is the only guard available -- add it in both files.
  - The completion ring's own layout is guarded by the guest/host asserts above,
    not by the passage-page assertions (it is not in the passage page).


11. Residual risks and things to watch
---------------------------------------

  - preempt_disable across a crossing is unchanged from today and remains
    correct: it prevents the guest scheduler from interleaving two tasks'
    param writes on the single guest CPU. The async path still writes params
    under preempt_disable and the host still copies before returning.
  - blk_mq_end_request from sysvec_co_timer runs in interrupt context; confirm
    on first hardware run that no cobd completion path assumes process context
    (it should not; the request's bio endio work is deferred appropriately).
    If any lockdep/context complaint appears, switch to the blk_mq_complete_
    request + .complete-op form (3.4) which steers completion to softirq.
  - The FILE_SYNCHRONOUS_IO_NONALERT handle serializes per unit; this is fine
    for the fix and is called out so no one is surprised that adding workers to
    one unit does not speed it up (3.8).
  - Worker joins can be delayed up to one in-flight ZwReadFile at stop; this is
    bounded and is strictly better than the current inline behaviour (which
    blocks the guest for the WHOLE queue). Confirm stop.bat and driver unload
    latency remain acceptable.
  - Keep the heartbeat honest: route block_requests/block_errors counting
    through the worker/complete path so the "hb: ... blkio" line and
    out->block_* still reflect reality once I/O moved off the monitor thread.


